# Shell scripts keep LF endings, whatever core.autocrlf says.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to autocrlf=true, which rewrites these to CRLF on
# checkout. A shebang whose line ends in a carriage return is a script that
# dies on its very first line - "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" -
# before it can print anything useful. That is a bad way for `install.sh` to
# meet somebody on a machine they are setting up, and this folder is handed
# over as often on macOS as on Windows.
#
# The .bat files are the other way round on purpose and are left alone:
# cmd.exe expects CRLF.
*.sh  text eol=lf
